WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.240
JENNY BROWN: In
our previous video,

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:02.670
we looked at how
enable broadening

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:04.200
and disable keyword
search can help

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.617
you adjust the
number of jobs that

00:00:05.617 --> 00:00:07.090
are returned from a search.

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:08.730
Let's talk about
two more options,

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:10.560
featured jobs and
custom ranking,

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.450
to further influence
your search results.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:15.168
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:19.200
First up, you have the
ability to feature a job

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.010
by setting a promotion value.

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:22.710
This is a property
actually attached

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:24.900
to each individual job posting.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.390
When performing a search
request against the API,

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:30.480
you can filter if you want only
featured jobs to be returned.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.549
Using featured jobs
allows you to highlight

00:00:32.549 --> 00:00:35.790
jobs that are important in
terms of your business needs.

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:39.000
Any featured jobs are returned
in order of promotion value.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.130
If multiple jobs have
the same promotion value,

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:43.920
the relevance of the job
listing is used as a tiebreaker.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.170
However, it's important to
balance not only how you're

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.780
showing featured jobs, but
also ensuring the relevance

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.260
of search results.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:51.890
If you only show
featured jobs which

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:53.667
are ordered by your
promotion value,

00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:55.500
you may not be returning
the highest quality

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.420
matches for a search request.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:59.790
This means that the user
may not be exactly seeing

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:02.769
the best experience and
could have lower engagement.

00:01:02.769 --> 00:01:04.769
So it's important to
balance your implementation

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:08.040
with the number and
presentation of featured jobs.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:10.140
Also, when searching
for featured jobs

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:11.730
you can use enable
broadening, so you

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:13.830
have expanded query
results that come back

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:15.240
when doing a search request.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.980
One important note
is that we recommend

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.680
not using featured jobs when
disable keyword match is

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:20.778
set to false.

00:01:20.778 --> 00:01:22.320
This can lead to a
featured job being

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.720
promoted when it could be fairly
irrelevant to the user's query.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:27.690
For example, if
you featured a job

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:30.360
for a software engineer
with a server administration

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.160
in the description
and someone searches

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:33.900
for a restaurant
server, they may

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.930
see this software engineering
job as the top match.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.750
With keyword matching,
this job fits the criteria,

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.450
and coupled with being featured,
it might end up towards the top

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.940
and provide a poor
experience for the user.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:46.650
Again, it's important
to make sure

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:49.230
the configuration makes
sense for your use cases

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:51.170
and that you test to
get the best results.

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:53.258
Featured jobs lets you
split off certain jobs

00:01:53.258 --> 00:01:55.050
to be returned separately,
but you can also

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:57.600
use custom ranking to influence
the order in which jobs

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:58.530
are returned.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.780
Custom ranking can be done
through an expression where

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:02.370
you create a
formula to influence

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.050
the ordering of results.

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:06.360
These formulas can involve
simple math and custom

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.400
attributes for jobs, which
we'll talk a bit more

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:09.900
about in the next video.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.603
When setting a custom
ranking formula,

00:02:11.603 --> 00:02:13.020
the jobs in the
search result will

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:14.940
get returned according
to your formula,

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:17.190
giving you more control
over ordering them.

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:19.710
Based on the way you want
jobs to return and to keep

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:22.560
job search results relevant,
you should use featured jobs

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:23.820
or custom ranking.

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:25.350
However, we don't
really recommend

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:26.875
using both at the same time.

00:02:26.875 --> 00:02:28.500
They're both powerful
tools to give you

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:31.080
the customization support
to meet your business logic,

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:33.400
but they're two ways of
solving the same problem.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:34.890
So using them at
the same time can

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.930
be hard to manage and
give confusing results

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.320
for your users.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.260
Featured jobs and
customer ranking,

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.530
along with broadening
and keyword matching,

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:44.220
are major ways to
configure and tweak

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:47.010
the results that come back
from the job search API.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.980
In our next video, we'll look at
some more configuration options

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:52.050
that are a bit smaller,
and best practices

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.750
to keep in mind when you're
testing out your integration.

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:56.400
Thanks for watching,
and remember,

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.960
when looking for talent, it's OK
to keep your head in the cloud.

